<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>1.5.2 Graphing Linear Functions in Two Variables</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00121</md:content-id>
    <md:title>1.5.2 Graphing Linear Functions in Two Variables</md:title>
    <md:uuid>5d971ba9-9b99-45c8-8412-a9ffe23a2738</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Activity</title>
<para/>
<title>Launch </title>
<para>Give each student a piece of graph paper or a coordinate grid. Have students recall how to plot a point with the \(x\)-value before the \(y\)-value.</para>
<para>Arrange students in pairs.</para>
<para>Encourage students to check with their partner at each step to compare their work before moving on to the next step. Have students explain their steps to their partner.</para>
<title>Student Activity</title>
<para>In this activity, students will work to graph equations written in standard form using the \(x\)- and \(y\)-intercepts by hand, not technology. They will then use the graphs to find the slope of the lines. </para>

 <para> The teacher may choose to begin to associate the slope of the equation of the line in this form to \(-\frac{A}{B}\). Students will later graph these equations using graphing technology in lesson 1.10, so it is helpful for students to understand how to complete the process on their own.</para>
<para>For questions 1 - 7, use the equation \(3x + 4y =12\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item> Find the value of \(y\) when \(x = 0\). </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> 3</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item> Find the value of \(x\) when \(y = 0\). </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> 4</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item> Plot both of those points on a coordinate grid and use a ruler to draw a line. </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis></para>
<figure><media alt="A plot of the x and y coordiantes of the equation 3x + 4y =12."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/964b7422a6bddf9a6da3d460b9aebccd439aec35"/></media></figure><list class="os-raise-noindent" start="4" type="enumerated">
  <item> What is the coordinate of the \(x\)-intercept? </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>\((4,0)\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="5" type="enumerated">
  <item> What is the coordinate of the \(y\)-intercept? </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>\((0,3)\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="6" type="enumerated">
  <item> What are two other names for the \(x\)-intercept? </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> zero, solution</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="7" type="enumerated">
  <item> What is the slope of the line? </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(-\frac34\)</para>
<title>Activity Synthesis </title>
<para>Facilitate a discussion with students with them explaining how they found their answers.</para>
<list>
  <item> Ask: “ How did you find your \(x\)-intercepts and \(y\)-intercepts?” </item>
  <item> Ask: “How did you find your slope?“ </item>
  <item> Ask: “Why do you think an \(x\)-intercept is also called a zero or solution?” </item>
  <item>Ask: “What did you notice about the numbers in the equation and the numbers in the intercepts and slopes?“</item>
</list>
<para>If time allows, try another example and ask the questions again and reflect if their analysis was accurate. </para>
<title>1.5.2: Self Check </title>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold"><emphasis>After the activity, students will answer the following question to check their understanding of the concepts explored in the activity.</emphasis></para>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold">QUESTION:</para>
<para>Graph the equation \(5x-2y= 10\). Which of the following describes the intercepts and slope of the graph?</para>
<table class="os-raise-textheavytable">
  <thead>
    <row>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">Answers</entry>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">Feedback</entry>
    </row>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <row>
      <entry><para>\(x\)-intercept: \(x=2\)</para>
        <para>\(y\)-intercept: \(y= -5\)</para>
        <para>Slope: \(\frac25\)</para></entry>
      <entry><para>Incorrect. Let’s try again a different way: The intercepts are correct, but the slope is the reciprocal of what it should be. The answer is:</para>
        <para>\(x\)-intercept: \(x=2\)</para>
        <para>\(y\)-intercept: \(y= -5\)</para>
        <para>Slope: \(\frac52\)</para></entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry><para>\(x\)-intercept: \(x= -5\)</para>
        <para>\(y\)-intercept: \(y= 2\)</para>
        <para>Slope: \(\frac52\)</para></entry>
      <entry><para>Incorrect. Let’s try again a different way: The intercept values are switched. The answer is:</para>
        <para>\(x\)-intercept: \(x=2\)</para>
        <para>\(y\)-intercept: \(y= -5\)</para>
        <para>Slope: \(\frac52\)</para></entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry><para>\(x\)-intercept: \(x=2\)</para>
        <para>\(y\)-intercept: \(y= -5\)</para>
        <para>Slope: \(\frac52\)</para></entry>
      <entry><para>That’s correct! Check yourself: When \(y=0\), \(x=2\). When \(x=0\), \(y=-5\). On the graph, the rise over run will be up 5 and right 2 for a slope of \(\frac52\).</para></entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry><para>\(x\)-intercept: \(x=-2\)</para>
        <para>\(y\)-intercept: \(y= 5\)</para>
        <para>Slope: \(-\frac52\)</para></entry>
      <entry><para>Incorrect. Let’s try again a different way: Be careful with signs when solving for a variable. The answer is:</para>
        <para>\(x\)-intercept: \(x=2\)</para>
        <para>\(y\)-intercept: \(y= -5\)</para>
        <para>Slope: \(\frac52\)</para></entry>
    </row>
  </tbody>
</table>
<title>1.5.2: Additional Resources </title>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold"><emphasis>The following content is available to students who would like more support based on their experience with the self check. Students will not automatically have access to this content, so you may wish to share it with those who could benefit from it.</emphasis></para>
<title>Graphing a Line and Describing Characteristics</title>
<para>There are many ways to graph an equation of a line. One way is to use the \(x\)-intercepts and \(y\)-intercepts as two points on the line.</para>
<para><emphasis>Example</emphasis></para>
<para>Graph \(3x -6y =18\) using the intercepts, then identify key characteristics.</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Find the \(x\)-intercept.</para>
<para>\(\begin{array}{rcl}3x -6y &amp;=&amp;18\\3x -6(0) &amp;=&amp;18\\3x&amp;=&amp;18\\x&amp;=&amp;6\end{array}\)</para>
<para>\((6,0)\) is the \(x\)-intercept</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Find the \(y\)-intercept.</para>
<para>\(\begin{array}{rcl}3x -6y &amp;=&amp;18\\3(0) -6y &amp;=&amp;18\\-6y &amp;=&amp;18\\y&amp;=&amp;-3\end{array}\)</para>
<para>\((0, -3)\) is the \(y\)-intercept</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Graph the line by connecting the \(y\)-intercepts.</para>
<figure><media alt="A plot of y intercepts of the equation 3x - 6y =18."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/dcb5cbbdfbf1702114c941d4ee755a0c53574254"/></media></figure><para><emphasis>Step 4</emphasis> - Find the slope of the line.</para>
<para>\(\mathrm{slope}=\frac{\mathrm{rise}}{\mathrm{run}}=\frac36=\frac12\)</para>
<title>Try It: Graphing a Line and Describing Characteristics</title>
<para>For 1 - 2, use the equation \(7x - 3y = 21\).</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item> Graph the equation using the intercepts. </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis></para><para><figure><media alt="Graph of the equation 7x - 3y = 21."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/fbd309b1df97a26f4dafbf66fd19e96d622f9708"/></media></figure></para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item> What is the slope of the line? </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(\frac73\) </para></section>
</content>
